<GuideLayout
  title="Planning an overnight hike in the Blue Mountains"
  seoTitle="Overnight Hikes in the Blue Mountains: Routes and Fire Rules"
  pageImage={guideImage}
  pageImageAlt="Grose River near Blue Gum Forest in the Grose Valley, Blue Mountains"
  description="Overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains: beginner-friendly routes, walk-in campsites, water, booking and fire season rules for planning your first night out."
  eyebrow="Where to go"
  readTime="11 min read"
>
  <p>
    The Blue Mountains is the obvious first overnight hike for most Sydney
    beginners: an hour or two by car or train, sandstone cliffs and eucalypt
    valleys instead of suburbs, and a handful of walk-in campsites that don't
    demand alpine gear or a week off work.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is also easy to get wrong. Some of these tracks descend hundreds of
    metres of steep, exposed steps that feel very different with a full pack on
    your back, and NSW's fire season closes tracks and restricts stoves for
    months at a time. This guide covers where to go, in a sensible order for a
    first-timer, plus the booking and fire rules, and stays out of the cooking and gear detail that <a href="/guides/hiking-stove-guide/"
      >our stove guide</a
    > and <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">packing list</a> already
    cover.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Acacia Flat, Ingar and Kedumba River Crossing campgrounds need an online
        booking (no camping fee, but a $6 booking fee). The old Perrys Lookdown
        campground has been closed to camping since the 2019 fires.
      </li>
      <li>
        A simple beginner ladder: test your tent, mat and sleeping bag at Euroka
        campground (drive-in), then Ingar (about 5km each way on fire trail), then
        Acacia Flat (short but Grade 4), then Kedumba, then the Six Foot Track.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Six Foot Track (Katoomba to Jenolan) is a 42km, three-day step up
        for once you have an overnight hike under your belt, and currently ends
        at Binda Bush cabins while Jenolan Caves is closed.
      </li>
      <li>
        NSW's Bush Fire Danger Period runs statewide from 1 October, and on a
        declared Total Fire Ban day, open fires and solid-fuel cooking are
        banned outright, and NPWS says visitor-owned gas cookers are generally
        prohibited too. NPWS park fire bans can also ban wood and solid-fuel
        (hexi) stoves for months at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Always check the specific track's NPWS alerts page and the RFS Total
        Fire Ban status the afternoon before you leave.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figure class="guide-photo">
    <Image
      src={guideImage}
      alt="Grose River near Blue Gum Forest in the Grose Valley, Blue Mountains"
      widths={[360, 720, 1024, 1400]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 680px"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Photo: <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grose_River,_near_Blue_Gum_Forest,_Grose_Valley,_Blue_Mountains_1.JPG"
        rel="noopener"
        >Grose River, near Blue Gum Forest, Grose Valley, Blue Mountains</a
      > by Rangasyd, <a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/"
        rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a
      >, via Wikimedia Commons (resized).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    "Best" depends on your fitness and how comfortable you are on steep, uneven
    track. The routes below are all inside Blue Mountains National Park and are
    listed roughly from shorter or simpler to harder, through to a serious
    three-day walk.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Route</th><th>Distance</th><th>Booking needed?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Ingar campground (Woodford / Wentworth Falls area)</td>
            <td>About 5km each way on fire trail from Murphys Rd, Woodford (about 10km from Woodford station)</td>
            <td>Yes, online</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Perrys Lookdown to Acacia Flat (Grade 4)</td>
            <td>3.3km each way to Acacia Flat (6.6km return), grade 4 (hard), very steep with many steps</td>
            <td>Acacia Flat campground: yes, $6 booking fee, no camping fee</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Kedumba River Crossing campground</td>
            <td>9km one-way via Kedumba Pass from Wentworth Falls</td>
            <td>Yes, online</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Six Foot Track (Katoomba to Binda Bush cabins)</td>
            <td>42km one-way, usually 3 days</td>
            <td
              >No park booking for camping; cabins/transport arranged separately</td
            >
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Distances and grades from NSW National Parks and hiker trip reports.
      Check current alerts before you go; tracks and campgrounds close for
      weather, fire and maintenance.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Which Blue Mountains overnight hike should I do first?</h2>
  <p>
    If you've never camped out of a pack before, work up in steps rather than
    jumping straight to the hardest walk:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Test your kit before you walk in.</strong> Set up your tent, mat and
      sleeping bag at home or on a day trip so the first overnight is not your
      first setup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Euroka campground.</strong> A drive-in car-camping test night near
      Glenbrook, with a short carry to the tent area and no water, so bring all
      your water and book online.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ingar campground.</strong> A moderate fire-trail walk, easy to
      navigate, with a steep drop to Bedford Creek, reachable from Woodford
      station, and a swimming hole at the end.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Acacia Flat via Perrys Lookdown.</strong> 3.3km each way, but Grade
      4: a steep descent and a hard climb back out with a full pack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kedumba River Crossing.</strong> Longer, with a long, steep climb
      out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Six Foot Track.</strong> Three days, once you have a few
      nights behind you.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Euroka campground: a drive-in gear-test night</h2>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/euroka-campground"
      rel="noopener">Euroka campground</a
    > near Glenbrook is drive-in car camping, not a hike. You may need to carry
    your gear a short distance from the car to the tent area, so it is useful for
    testing a tent, mat, sleeping bag and stove without making the first night a
    walking challenge. NPWS lists toilets and barbecues, but no water, so bring
    all your water and book online. Kangaroos can be seen, but sightings are not
    guaranteed. NPWS currently lists Euroka as exempt from the Blue Mountains
    National Park fire ban; check the current <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/euroka-campground/local-alerts"
      rel="noopener">Euroka alerts</a
    > before relying on that date-sensitive exemption.
  </p>

  <h2>Ingar campground: a moderate first walk-in overnighter</h2>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/ingar-campground"
      rel="noopener">Ingar campground</a
    > sits south of Woodford and Wentworth Falls. It's about 5km each way on
    fire trail from Murphys Rd, Woodford, or about 10km in from Woodford
    station if you come by train. The trail is easy to follow, but NPWS says
    both access trails have steep sections and can become inaccessible in wet
    weather. Hikers report an ankle-deep crossing of Bedford Creek about 2.6km
    in. The camp has
    eight unmarked tent sites, toilets and picnic tables, and a waterhole people
    swim in. There's no tap water, so treat water from the waterhole (tablets
    are the cheapest option) or carry your own. It needs an online booking.
  </p>

  <h2>Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest and Acacia Flat: a steep step up</h2>
  <p>
    This is a steep second-trip route, not a beginner option. NPWS lists the Blue
    Gum Forest walk as a 4km return, grade 4 walk of 3 to 4 hours, and the walk
    down to Acacia Flat as 3.3km each way. It drops steeply
    from the Perrys Lookdown car park
    near Blackheath through a cliff line into the Grose Valley to reach the
    historic <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/perrys-lookdown-to-blue-gum-forest-walking-track"
      rel="noopener">Blue Gum Forest</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Grade 4" on the NPWS scale means a steep, challenging track with many
    steps, and it feels harder again with an overnight pack on the way back up.
    Map data suggests the track drops about 350m to the forest, so expect the
    climb out to be the slow part with an overnight pack. Leave early to avoid
    the heat, and trekking poles help a lot on the steps. Acacia Flat campground, near the forest, needs an <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/acacia-flat-campground"
      rel="noopener">online booking</a
    > and charges a small booking fee rather than a nightly camping fee. There is
    no tap water, but you can treat creek water (Orang Utan Gully, at the
    southern end of the camp, is the usual pick); carry a backup supply in case
    it's low. Only fuel stoves are permitted there, not open campfires. Expect
    leeches after rain, and no phone reception in the valley.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no camping at Perrys Lookdown itself: the old car-park campground
    has been closed since the 2019 fires and the site is now day use only. Book
    Acacia Flat if you want to sleep in the valley, or use Euroka for a drive-in
    gear-test night.
  </p>

  <h2>Kedumba River Crossing: a step up</h2>
  <p>
    Kedumba River Crossing campground is the longer, harder option near
    Wentworth Falls, and needs an online booking. It's about 9km one-way down the
    steep Kedumba Pass from Wentworth Falls, with 20 sites beside the river.
    NPWS itself describes the climb back out as "extremely steep and
    challenging" and says it takes 3 to 4 hours, so this one suits hikers who
    already have an overnight trip behind them rather than a first attempt.
    Hikers report about 3 hours in and 3.5 hours out on steep fire trail, and
    you may have the camp to yourself midweek in winter. Several tracks around
    Katoomba and Wentworth Falls are closed for rockfall or landslide, so check
    NPWS alerts before planning any loop that uses the Mount Solitary track.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Allow 3 to 4 hours for the Kedumba Pass climb out of the valley. Treat that
    as the normal time for your return leg, not a worst case.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Is the Six Foot Track a good overnight hike for beginners?</h2>
  <p>
    Not as a first trip. The Six Foot Track runs 42km one-way from Katoomba and
    is usually walked over three days, which means two nights of self-supported
    camping and carrying more food and water than a single overnight hike. It is
    worth knowing about once you have one or two shorter overnight hikes under
    your belt. Currently the track ends at Binda Bush cabins rather than
    continuing to Jenolan Caves, because the <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/six-foot-track"
      rel="noopener">Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed</a
    > until at least the end of 2026; check the NPWS alerts on that page before you plan transport, since there
    is no public transport to or from Binda Bush cabins.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the fire season rules for Blue Mountains overnight hikes?</h2>
  <p>
    NSW's statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October, and it is the
    backdrop for almost every spring and summer overnight hike in the Blue
    Mountains. During that period, and especially on a day the NSW Rural Fire
    Service declares a Total Fire Ban, the rules tighten:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      On a Total Fire Ban day you cannot light, maintain or use an open fire,
      and solid-fuel cooking (wood, charcoal, heat-bead barbecues) is banned
      outright, according to the <a
        href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/total-fire-ban-rules"
        rel="noopener">NSW RFS Total Fire Ban rules</a
      >. NPWS also lists liquid fuel (such as methylated spirits) and hexamine
      stoves as prohibited in national parks on those days.
    </li>
    <li>
      NSW National Parks says visitor-owned gas cookers are generally prohibited
      on a Total Fire Ban day unless the park office agrees in advance, so don't
      assume your gas stove is fine.
    </li>
    <li>
      NPWS also declares its own park fire bans, separate from RFS Total Fire
      Bans. These can ban all campfires and wood or solid-fuel (hexi) stoves
      across the park for months, which catches out people with cheap wood or
      hexi stoves.
    </li>
    <li>
      For 2026-27, NPWS has a Blue Mountains National Park fire ban from 1
      October 2026 to 31 March 2027: no campfires or solid-fuel stoves, and gas
      stoves only with precautions (2m of cleared ground, water on hand).
    </li>
    <li>
      NPWS currently lists Euroka as exempt from that park fire ban. Check the
      <a
        href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/euroka-campground/local-alerts"
        rel="noopener">Euroka local alerts</a
      > before you rely on a date-sensitive exemption.
    </li>
    <li>
      NPWS can and does close individual tracks and campgrounds for fire risk
      independently of a Total Fire Ban, so a park being open last week doesn't
      mean it's open this week.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because this can change day to day, treat it as a final check rather than
    something to plan around weeks out: look up the specific track's NPWS alerts
    page and the current NSW RFS fire ban status the afternoon before you leave,
    and have a fallback plan if either says no. Our <a
      href="/guides/hiking-stove-guide/">hiking stove guide</a
    > covers stove types and fuel in more depth if you're still choosing gear.
  </p>

  <h2>How do I book an NSW National Parks campsite for an overnight hike?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Most Blue Mountains walk-in campgrounds, including Acacia Flat, Ingar and
      Kedumba River Crossing, are booked online through the <a
        href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/camping-in-national-parks"
        rel="noopener">NSW National Parks booking system</a
      >, with a small booking fee rather than a nightly camping charge.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can also book by phone through the National Parks Contact Centre on
      1300 072 757 (7am to 7pm daily).
    </li>
    <li>
      A few sites, such as the Six Foot Track campsites, are not booked through
      NPWS, so check the track page and the land manager for the rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tell someone your plans and when you expect to be back; there's no phone
      reception in the valley.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gear do you need for an overnight hike in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    The routes above don't need anything exotic, but the steep, rocky descents
    reward a pack that actually fits and gear you've tested before you're 4km
    from the car with a sore back. Our <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike packing list</a
    > covers the full kit list, and if you're still choosing a sleeping system, start
    with <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
      >sleeping bag temperature ratings</a
    > and <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">sleeping mat R-value</a>,
    since Blue Mountains nights get cold even when Sydney doesn't. Our <a
      href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a
    > bundle a pack, sleep system and shelter together, or use <a
      href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a
    > to fill gaps in gear you already own.
  </p>
  <p>
    For routes in other states, see our <a
      href="/guides/best-multi-day-hikes-australia/"
      >best multi-day hikes in Australia</a
    > guide.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Start small: test your kit at home, then use Euroka as a drive-in gear-test
    night or take a moderate walk into Ingar before taking on the steep walk down
    to Acacia Flat once your gear and legs are sorted. Book your campsite online
    ahead of time, save the Six Foot Track for once
    you've done a night or two, and always check NPWS alerts and the NSW RFS
    fire ban status the day before you leave, since both can change the plan at
    short notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best overnight hike near Sydney for beginners?</summary
      >
      <p>
        In the Blue Mountains, Ingar campground is a moderate first walk-in trip:
        about 5km each way on fire trail from Murphys Rd, Woodford, and reachable
        from the station. Perrys Lookdown to Acacia Flat is shorter (3.3km each
        way) but Grade 4 and much steeper, so it suits a second trip. For a
        no-walking test night, try Euroka campground near Glenbrook. For an
        easier walk-in trip, see Uloola Falls in Royal National Park in our <a
          href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-nsw/">NSW guide</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Do I need to book a campsite for an overnight hike in the Blue
        Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        For most walk-in NPWS campgrounds, including Acacia Flat, Ingar and
        Kedumba River Crossing, yes, you book online in advance. A few sites,
        such as the Six Foot Track campsites, are not booked through NPWS.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long is the Six Foot Track?</summary>
      <p>
        The Six Foot Track is 42km one-way and is usually walked over three
        days. It currently finishes at Binda Bush cabins rather than Jenolan
        Caves while the Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Can I use a gas stove during a total fire ban in the Blue Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Not reliably. NSW National Parks says visitor-owned gas cookers are
        generally prohibited on a Total Fire Ban day unless the park office agrees
        in advance, and open fires, liquid fuel and solid fuel stoves are
        prohibited. Check the current RFS fire ban status and the track's NPWS
        alerts, and carry a no-cook meal.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>When does NSW's fire season affect Blue Mountains hikes?</summary
      >
      <p>
        The statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October. Total Fire
        Bans can be declared on individual high-risk days within and sometimes
        outside that period, and NPWS park fire bans can ban wood and
        solid-fuel stoves for months, so check status close to your trip date.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there water at Acacia Flat campground?</summary>
      <p>
        There's no tap water, but you can treat creek water from Orang Utan
        Gully at the southern end of the camp with tablets, a filter or by
        boiling. Carry a backup supply in case it's low.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is Jenolan Caves currently open?</summary>
      <p>
        No. The Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed until at least the
        end of 2026, with reopening targeted for early 2027, and the Six Foot
        Track ends at Binda Bush cabins instead of continuing to
        the caves. Recheck the NPWS track page before planning transport, as
        this may change.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
